cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Value-Field Theory (VFT)"
message: "If you use this theory or repository, please cite this repository."
authors:
  - family-names: "Nuno"
    given-names: "T."
    affiliation: "Independent Researcher"
abstract: >
  Value-Field Theory (VFT) is a structural framework for describing how a
  shared multidimensional real-resource world K, actor-specific accessible
  portions K_i, and actor-specific expectation-forming states P_i condition
  actions and decisions A_i. Delta K(S, tau) denotes the realized real-resource
  quantity difference over a defined target scope S and interval tau. The same
  Delta K type is used across micro and macro scales; what changes is the actor
  set, time horizon, resource scope, and aggregation rule. E_i[Delta K(S, tau)]
  denotes an actor-specific expected resource change where the relevant Delta K
  is represented on a quantitative projection for which an expectation is
  defined. Preferences, desirability, credit, beliefs, and related structures
  remain in P_i rather than being identified with E_i[Delta K]. In economic
  projections, K_i may represent budget, resource, or production constraints,
  while P_i may contain expectations about prices, other actors, and future
  conditions. Actor-specific expected production and consumption can then be
  treated as signed E_i[Delta K] terms. Under a common scope, horizon, accounting
  rule, and closed-boundary assumptions, their consistency may be represented by
  an expectation-side equilibrium such as sum_i E_i[Delta K] approximately zero;
  boundary terms such as inventories, imports, exports, or exogenous flows must
  be included when relevant. Price, trade, contract, inventory, and credit
  observables can update P_i and thereby support decentralized adjustment of
  expectations and actions. This adjustment may be projected to the classical
  invisible-hand idea, without implying that equilibrium is necessarily socially
  optimal. Mapping aggregate expected resource change to standard economic
  surplus requires additional valuation, utility, WTP/WTA, or cost assumptions.
repository-code: "https://github.com/nuno929/vf-model"
